{
  "title": "Mi {ip:piace} il caffè e lo {ip:bevo} sempre",
  "translation": "I like coffee and I always drink it",
  "topics": "Direct object pronouns, piacere and sapere",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      "A direct object pronoun replaces a noun and answers *whom?* or *what?*. It comes before the verb: *{ip:Compro} il pane. → Lo {ip:compro}.*",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Pronoun",
            "Meaning",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "mi",
              "me",
              "Mi {ip:chiami} domani?"
            ],
            [
              "ti",
              "you",
              "Ti {ip:vedo} stasera."
            ],
            [
              "lo",
              "him, it (masc.)",
              "Il libro? Lo {ip:leggo}."
            ],
            [
              "la",
              "her, it (fem.)",
              "La pizza? La {ip:mangio}."
            ],
            [
              "ci",
              "us",
              "Ci {ip:aspetti}?"
            ],
            [
              "vi",
              "you (plural)",
              "Vi {ip:invito}."
            ],
            [
              "li",
              "them (masc.)",
              "I libri? Li {ip:leggo}."
            ],
            [
              "le",
              "them (fem.)",
              "Le chiavi? Le {ip:prendo}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Non* goes before the pronoun: *Non lo {ip:bevo}.*",
      "*Piacere* works differently from *to like*: the thing you like is the subject. Use *{ip:piace}* with one thing or a verb, and *{ip:piacciono}* with several things.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Who likes",
            "One thing or a verb",
            "Several things"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "I",
              "Mi {ip:**piace**} il caffè.",
              "Mi {ip:**piacciono**} i gatti."
            ],
            [
              "you",
              "Ti {ip:**piace**} leggere?",
              "Ti {ip:**piacciono**} i film?"
            ],
            [
              "he / she",
              "Gli / Le {ip:**piace**} Roma.",
              "Gli / Le {ip:**piacciono**} i fiori."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Sapere* means *to know* (a fact), and with an infinitive *to know how to*: *{ip:So} nuotare* (I can swim).",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "io",
            "tu",
            "lui / lei",
            "noi",
            "voi",
            "loro"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "so",
              "{ip:sai}",
              "{ip:sa}",
              "{ip:sappiamo}",
              "{ip:sapete}",
              "{ip:sanno}"
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
